Q: std::copy_assignable_v not working for reference types, how to fix? A: <p><code>is_copy_assignable&lt;T&gt;</code> is shorthand for <code>is_assignable&lt;T&amp;, T const&amp;&gt;</code> : <a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/type.traits#tab:meta.unary.prop-row-22-column-2-sentence-1" rel="nofollow noreferrer">link</a>.</p>
<p>The <code>T</code> above can be an lvalue-reference type, so it is also true that:</p>
<p><code>is_copy_assignable&lt;T&amp;&gt;</code> is shorthand for <code>is_assignable&lt;T&amp;, T const&amp;&gt;</code></p>
<p>(An lvalue-reference to an lvalue-reference collapses to an lvalue-reference) : <a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/dcl.ref#7" rel="nofollow noreferrer">link</a>.</p>
<p><code>is_assiganble</code> can be thought of as an &quot;expression-based&quot; trait. The design is that lvalue reference types represent lvalue expressions and value or rvalue reference types represent rvalue expressions.</p>
<p>So <code>is_assignable&lt;T&amp;, T const&amp;&gt;</code> should be interpreted as:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Is an lvalue expression of type <code>T</code> assignable from an lvalue expression of type <code>T const</code>?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>In your example <code>T</code> is <code>int&amp;</code> so (with reference collapsing):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Is an lvalue <code>int</code> assignable from an lvalue <code>int const</code>?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The answer is yes.</p>
<p>However when one has a lvalue reference data member of a struct/class, the rules are that the copy assignment operator is deleted: <a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/class.copy.assign#7.2" rel="nofollow noreferrer">link</a>.</p>
<p>This is the line in the standard that dictates your <code>false</code> result for <code>IntProxy</code>.</p>
<p>Yes, this is an inconsistency in the language.  This inconsistency predates the <code>&lt;type_traits&gt;</code> library, which strives to be consistent and just tells it like it is.  This is meant to be a reliable aid to generic code:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;type_traits&gt;

template &lt;class T&gt;
requires (std::is_copy_assignable_v&lt;T&gt;)
auto
test(T t, T u)
{
    t = u;
    return t;
}

struct IntProxy
{
    int&amp; i;
};

int main()
{
    int j = 0;
    int&amp; i = j;
    test(i, j);  // compiles

    IntProxy k{j};
    IntProxy m{j};
    test(k, m);  // fails to compile
}
</code></pre>
